ATTEMPT-19 RUNTIME FIX APPLIED TO E1-0'S OWN FILE (not to any original file)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`e10_sglang_gpu_patch.py` previously executed
`monkey_patch_torch_reductions()` at MODULE IMPORT time. Because sitecustomize
imports that module in every process (including the Ray TaskRunner driver),
this applied sglang's CUDA-IPC reduction patch to the driver as well, which
deadlocks fork-based `StatefulDataLoader` workers: the patched `reduce_tensor`
maps a serialized tensor's device through `torch.cuda.get_device_properties()`,
i.e. CUDA work inside a forked child, so the driver waited forever for its
first validation batch and the 8 workers waited forever for an index. That was
the real cause of the silent hangs in attempts 14/16/17/18 and this session's
19a (driver stack: `stateful_dataloader._try_get_data`; worker stack:
`torchdata...worker._worker_loop -> index_queue.get()`; SIGUSR1 stacks in
logs/attempt19a_driver_and_dataloader_worker_SIGUSR1_stacks.txt).

Fix: the call is now deferred to `_ensure_monkey_patched()`, invoked from
`SGLangRollout._init_distributed_env`, which runs only inside the
WorkerDict/FSDP actor that actually serializes weights over CUDA IPC. The
receiving SGLang scheduler subprocesses already invoke the same function
themselves (sglang/srt/model_executor/model_runner.py:1295). File changed:
E1-0's own e10_sglang_gpu_patch.py. Full write-up:
analysis/root_cause_dataloader_fork.md.
